Translation patch: rename the six local bindings n_blocks· (U+00B7) in the hacl-generated hmac code to n_blocks_prev
Change: crates/algorithms/hmac/src/hacl/hmac.rs, compute_sha2_256 / compute_sha2_384 / compute_sha2_512 (two blocks in each, one in the inner hash over the data and one in the outer hash): in each of the six `let n_blocks·: u32 = n_blocks.wrapping_sub(1u32);` blocks the local binding `n_blocks·` (identifier ends in U+00B7 MIDDLE DOT) is renamed to `n_blocks_prev`, at its definition and at its two uses (`fst: ...` and the `wrapping_mul` operand). Eighteen lines in six hunks (three per block); no other token changes. No line is added, removed or moved.
Why: Aeneas copies the Rust local name into the generated Lean, and U+00B7 is not allowed inside a Lean identifier, so the generated file fails to parse at three places (970:24, 1307:24 and 1651:24 in the failed-extraction scratch build). The rename is the only change needed for these three parse errors.
Behaviour: pure alpha-renaming of a local `let` binding. Each binding is used only in the two statements of the same `if` arm that follow it; the new name `n_blocks_prev` does not occur anywhere else in the hmac crate (searched) and does not shadow or clash with `n_blocks`, `n_blocks0`, `rem`, `rem0`, `rem_len` or `scrut`. Types, values and evaluation order are untouched. Positions: the new name is four characters longer than the old one, so on these eighteen lines the columns of the tokens after the renamed name move by four; those tokens are `.wrapping_sub(..)` and `.wrapping_mul(..)` calls, the struct field list and punctuation, none of which can panic, so no panic location changes.
Classification: A-mechanical
Assumptions: none.
Obligations: none.
Independent: yes

